  "worked_solution": "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\u2026,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=0$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\pi $\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\pi =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}.\n$$",
